new economic data paint a grim picture  of March as the country came to a  standstill business executives tell  President Trump that a key component to  reopening the economy is a lot more  testing the governor of New York says  he's signing an executive order that  will require people to wear a face  covering in public I'm Shelby Holliday  and here are your coronavirus headlines  from The Wall Street Journal u.s.  industrial production fell five point  four percent in March  it's the biggest drop in measured  factory utility and mining production  since nineteen forty two retail sales  which include a measure of purchases at  stores bars and restaurants fell eight  point seven percent in March from a  month earlier it's the biggest  month-over-month drop since records  began in 1992 these historic lows are  the result of the pandemic affecting  factories malls restaurants and other  businesses weekly jobless numbers will  be released today and the number of  Americans seeking unemployment benefits  is expected to surpass 20 million  business leaders told President Trump  that his administration must  dramatically ramp up coronavirus testing  according to people familiar with the  matter the executives said that tests  were necessary to make Americans feel  confident enough to resume their normal  lives a White House spokesman declined  to comment on the conversation Trump  recently announced that executives from  companies like General Motors Apple and  McDonald's are all part of a new task  force to reopen the economy the  president is set to release new  guidelines today about restarting the  country by first opening up states with  the least coronavirus cases it will  ultimately be up to governor's to decide  when to open their states all people in  public must have a mass in the  hardest-hit u.s. state New York Governor  Andrew Cuomo announced a new facemask  rule that will kick in Friday people  will be required to wear face coverings  in public settings where they can come  into contact with others like in grocery  stores or when using public  transportation  coverings include medical masks as well  as bandanas or scarves Cuomo said there  could eventually be penalties for people  who don't comply with the order
 

